eir  businesses increased costs you've got to  understand that while that may all be  the inevitable way things will go in the  long term Trump said he was going to  reinvigorate the auto industry he was  going to bring back manufacturing jobs  and of course now you have a situation  where both Ford and GM are going to say  well a lot of this is restructuring it's  preparing for the future and that's all  true but it's all happening at the  expense of jobs which Trump said he  would bring back this is not a time for  tariffs this is a time for subsidies on  what we want these companies doing it's  time for subsidies on electric vehicles  subsidies on research for other fuels  and we've got to get rid of the  subsidies on oil help these companies  and then of course you have to remember  that a lot of the people that are being  hardest hit by this people in the  Midwest middle-class working-class white  people those that believe Trump was  going to be their guy they supported  Trump's election and they seem to be  still supporting Donald Trump and this  is sort of like the biggest takeaway we  all knew Trump didn't really know how to  bring back manufacturing jobs we knew  that he wasn't going to bring back  manufacturing jobs but what we're  learning is he doesn't actually seem to  care about the people that are losing  these jobs and the question is and we're  gonna address it on the Friday show are  we going to see working-class white  people in the Midwest move away from the  Republican Party that has been an  important demographic group for the  Republican Party and on the Friday show  we actually get an audience question  about which demographic groups do I  think might move away from the  Republican Party and I think that if  they start doing what's good for them  rural and and working class even urban  working class whites in the Midwest  really should be abandoning Trump in the  Republican Party we'll deal with that  more on Friday but in the interim really 